  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMCV moving into Indiana will continue to track eastward and cross \nthe forecast area today. This will bring periods of showers and \nthunderstorms. With precipitable water in the 1.8 to 2.0 inch range \nand warm cloud depths 3.5-4.0 km, very efficient rainfall rates will \nremain possible with any stronger convective elements. So, there will\nbe more locally heavy rainfall that could produce flash flooding. \nThere are signals within convective-allowing guidance that there \ncould be a pretty persistent west-east zone of showers and storms on \nthe northern edge of the convective system, which may be near or just\nsouth of the I-70 corridor. This could also be slightly enhanced by \na weak boundary in the vicinity.\n\nAll of this activity will diminish and push off to the southeast late\nthis afternoon into this evening as a short wave crosses the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \n\nLarge, anomalously strong mid level ridge centered over the northern\nPlains during the early part of the week will elongate and extend\ninto the region. This will bring drier and warmer conditions with\nWednesday still looking to be the warmest day. There is a fly in the\nointment before we get there. The short wave that is passing across\nthe area late today into tonight will be cutting off over the \nTennessee Valley early next week. An inverted trough extending \nnortheast from the associated surface low will extend across eastern \nKentucky into the upper Ohio Valley. So, isolated to scattered \ndiurnal convection will still be possible south and east of I-71 both\nSunday and Monday. After that point, the low will retrograde further\naway from the region as the high builds over top of it. \n\nTroughing over eastern Canada will develop into the northeastern US\nlate in the week, suppressing the ridge. As this occurs, the\npotential for diurnal convection will return. Also, with lower\nheights, temperatures will not be quite as high, although readings\nwill still be above normal.",
